<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="Account Read — Stress (distributed)" enabled="true">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Stress test: system-wide throughput on GET /api/v1/accounts/:id.

Virtual users are spread across MANY distinct accounts (tests/stress/data/accounts.csv,
1000 rows). Each VU iteration picks the next account via CSVDataSet round-robin
(shareMode.all + recycle), so single-row read contention is NOT a factor.
This isolates the system as a whole: Node event loop, Prisma connection pool,
Postgres query cache, network — whichever saturates first becomes the
breaking point.

Unlike the withdraw / deposit stress runs, this exercises the READ path:
  - No FOR UPDATE row lock (plain SELECT)
  - No $transaction interactive block
  - No write to the transactions table
  - Response payload is small and bounded

This is the lightest endpoint in the system, so the breaking point should
sit substantially higher than the write endpoints (withdraw 12 VUs, deposit
9 VUs from earlier runs). The interesting question is WHICH layer breaks
first when the write paths can no longer be the bottleneck.

Shape (Stepping Thread Group):
  - Start at 50 VUs (initial burst)
  - Add 50 VUs every 10s, ramp-up 1s per step
  - Peak at 2000 VUs (worst-case ramp ~6.5 min — breaking point typically arrives first)
  - Hold 60s at peak (only reached if system never broke)

Peak raised to 2000 VUs to find the realistic-pacing capacity ceiling
that 200 VUs failed to reveal. With the Gaussian timer each VU
generates ~0.3 req/s, so 2000 VUs aggregate to ~600 req/s — well
above the regime where Node event loop / Prisma pool behaviour
becomes observable. HTTP keepalive (enabled on the sampler) means
2000 concurrent VUs do NOT require 2000 ephemeral ports
simultaneously; the Windows BindException ceiling is unlikely to
fire as long as keepalive is honoured by the server.

Bursting 50 VUs at a time (instead of 1) keeps the ramp tractable —
200 single-VU steps × 10s would be 33 min just to reach peak.
40 × 50-VU steps × 10s = ~6:40 ramp + 60s hold + ~80s shutdown
= ~9 min total run. Shutdown burst (Stop users count) raised
symmetrically to 50 to avoid the otherwise absurd 4000s drain.

⚠ Load-generator memory: 2000 JVM threads at ~1 MB stack each is
~2 GB JMeter heap just for thread stacks. If JMeter starts swapping
or OOMs during the ramp, drop the peak (or invest the netsh / VU
tuning effort to drive higher load with fewer threads).

Pass criteria: no 5xx, no timeouts, all responses are 200.
Breaking point: first step where p95 &gt; 5000ms OR error rate &gt; 1%.

p95 is a report metric (Aggregate Report / HTML dashboard), not a per-sample
assertion. The only per-sample gate is the response-code assertion — any HTTP
failure or timeout counts toward the 1% error budget.

⚠ Windows load-generator caveat:
A GET account read is expected to return in &lt; 10 ms warm. At that latency
each VU can drive ~100 req/s, meaning even 10–15 VUs can push connection
churn past the Windows ephemeral-port ceiling (~68 conn/s with default
49152–65535 / 240s TIME_WAIT). If this run starts reporting
java.net.BindException before any p95 / 5xx breach, the breaking point
observed is the LOAD GENERATOR, not the server. See tests/stress/RESULTS.md
section 6 / E-1 for the Windows tuning fix
(`netsh int ipv4 set dynamicport tcp start=10000 num=55535` +
TcpTimedWaitDelay=30). Apply that before drawing conclusions about
server capacity from this run.

The response assertion is 200-only — there is no 422 path on a read.
A 403 (token / account mismatch) or 404 (missing account) would indicate
a data-fixture error in accounts.csv, not load behaviour, and must count
as a failure.

Think time: a Gaussian Random Timer is attached to the sampler (mean
3000ms, deviation 1000ms — total delay ≈ 3000ms + |N(0, 1000)| per
iteration). Deliberate choice: the goal is to measure capacity under
REALISTIC user pacing, not synthetic peak throughput. A no-think-time
stress test models a DDoS-style attacker; with the timer this measures
how many concurrent NORMAL users the system can sustain.

Consequence: each VU now generates ~0.3 req/s instead of ~100+ req/s
for fast reads. The BindException risk discussed above effectively
disappears (50 VUs × 0.3 req/s = 15 req/s — well under the ~68 conn/s
Windows ceiling). The earlier 3-5 VU no-timer "boundary" was almost
certainly load-generator-limited, not server-limited; this run should
walk cleanly to its peak. If the ramp completes without breaking,
raise the peak and re-run to actually find the realistic-pacing
capacity ceiling.

Run headless from PROJECT ROOT:
  jmeter -n -t tests/stress/jmeter/stress/account-read.stress.jmx \
         -l tests/stress/reports/account-read.jtl \
         -e -o tests/stress/reports/account-read-html

Or via the wrapper script:
  npm run test:stress:account-read

Prereqs:
  1. npx tsx tests/stress/scripts/seed.ts
  2. Server running on localhost:3000 (npm run build &amp;&amp; npm start)
  3. JMeter Plugin: jpgc-casutg (Custom Thread Groups)</stringProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.functional_mode">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups">false</boolProp>
      <elementProp name="TestPlan.user_defined_variables" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="ArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="User Defined Variables" enabled="true">
        <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
          <elementProp name="BASE_URL" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">BASE_URL</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(host,localhost)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="PORT" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">PORT</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(port,3000)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="DATA_DIR" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">DATA_DIR</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(dataDir,tests/stress/data)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
        </collectionProp>
      </elementProp>
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.user_define_classpath"></stringProp>
    </TestPlan>
